diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation of the ospf_enterprise_static lab found no active or historical fault consistent with the reported "core links bouncing" symptom.

**Affected component:** None — no subsystem, daemon, interface, or policy is misconfigured or impaired.

**Mechanism (why it would look the way the user described):** Link flapping would manifest as elevated carrier_change counters on router interfaces, repeated OSPF adjacency drops/re-establishments, and intermittent 100% loss in reachability probes. None of these traces are present.

**Localization:** All three core routers (router_core_1, router_core_2, router_core_3) have carrier_changes = 2 on every interface — the startup-only baseline for Kathara containers. dmesg on every core router shows only a single batch of bridge-port blocking→forwarding transitions at the container boot instant; no subsequent link-state events appear. All six OSPF-speaking devices maintain full adjacencies with all expected routes (10 per router). The l2_snapshot confirms every interface across all 15 devices is UP with no duplicate MACs. The pressure_sweep found zero stress-tool, flap-script, or flood processes on any device.

**Root cause:** No anomaly exists in the live lab — the topology is fully healthy and the reported intermittent outage left no mechanism, counter evidence, or log trace.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. The ticket should be closed as unconfirmed/resolved; if users continue to see problems, capture a contemporaneous tcpdump or OSPF log during the next occurrence to provide direct evidence of the fault.
=== END ===